Sri Lanka's money-app market has grown up fast. SMS-based capture works well, the local apps are genuinely good at what they do, and several are free.

But every one of them answers the same question, *where did my money go?*, and none of them can answer the two that actually decide anything: **what am I worth**, and **can I afford this?** Those need a balance sheet with your assets and liabilities on it, not a categorised transaction feed. [Here is why that distinction is structural rather than a missing feature](/blog/double-entry-explained-for-humans/).

Here's how the real options compare on published pricing and features as of 2026, including where each one stops.

## The field

| App | Automation | Sri Lankan bank SMS support | Tax engine | Price |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Salli** | AI quick-add from typed or spoken text | Manual entry / statement upload | Full IRD-aligned income tax engine (YA 2025/26) | Free tier; paid plans for tax + AI |
| **Kiwi Money** | Automatic: reads bank SMS | Commercial Bank, Sampath, HNB, BOC, NTB, Seylan, DFCC, HSBC, Standard Chartered, NSB, Genie, Dialog Finance | None | $0.99/week, $14.99/year, or $49.99 lifetime |
| **Money Master** | Automatic: reads bank SMS, on-device | 30+ Sri Lankan banks | None | Free |
| **Money Manager** (global) | Manual entry | None (not Sri Lanka-specific) | None | Free, with premium tier |
| **Goodbudget** (global) | Manual entry, envelope method | None | None | Free, with premium tier |
| **Spreadsheet** | Fully manual | N/A | None | Free |

*Sources: [Kiwi Money: Best Expense Tracker Apps in Sri Lanka (2026)](https://www.kiwi-money.com/comparisons/best-expense-tracker-app-sri-lanka-2026); [Kiwi Money official site](https://www.kiwi-money.com/); [Money Master Sri Lanka official site](https://moneymasterapp.com/).*

## What "automation" actually buys you

Kiwi Money and Money Master both solve the single biggest reason budgeting apps get abandoned: manual entry fatigue. Both read the SMS your bank sends when you spend, and log the transaction without you typing anything. Money Master does this entirely on-device: it operates 100% on-device with no server access to messages, which matters if you're wary of a third party reading your bank SMS traffic. Kiwi Money's version works the same way functionally but is a paid product with a 14-day trial.

Neither app, however, understands what any of those transactions mean for your Sri Lankan income tax. They'll tell you that you spent 4,500 at Keells. Neither will tell you whether that pattern of spending affects your APIT credit, your foreign-service income treatment, or your annual return.

## Where global apps fall short

Money Manager, Goodbudget, and AndroMoney are mature, widely-used products (Money Manager alone has over 10 million downloads globally), but none of them were built with Sri Lanka in mind. No local bank SMS parsing, no LKR-native design assumptions baked into categories, and certainly no awareness of IRD rate bands or APIT/AIT. For pure category-based budgeting they're fine. For anything tax-adjacent, you're on your own with a spreadsheet on the side regardless.

## Where Salli sits differently

Salli isn't trying to out-automate Kiwi Money or Money Master on SMS parsing. It starts from a different premise entirely: a real double-entry ledger, with a deterministic Sri Lankan tax engine sitting on top of it, and an AI layer that drafts entries from what you type or say but never touches the arithmetic.

That difference shows up concretely:

- **Every entry is double-entry**: auditable, immutable, and reconcilable, not just a categorised list.
- **The tax engine is the same one that would compute your actual IRD return**, covering relief, bands, APIT/AIT credits, and the 15% foreign-service rate, not a bolt-on estimate.
- **AI quick-add drafts the entry, and you approve it**, giving you the convenience of "just type it" without an opaque black box deciding your numbers.

The trade-off is that Salli asks a bit more of you up front (accounts, a real chart, an actual ledger) than an app that just reads your SMS and shows a pie chart. If all you want is "where did my money go this month," Kiwi Money or Money Master will get there faster. If you also want "what do I actually owe the IRD, and can I defend that number," none of the pure expense trackers get you there at all.

## Which one should you actually use?

A rough guide based on what you're optimising for:

- **Fastest to "just see my spending"**: Money Master (free, automated, no setup beyond linking SMS reading).
- **Willing to pay for a polished automated experience**: Kiwi Money.
- **Already comfortable in a spreadsheet and don't want an app**: keep doing that, but budget time each month to reconcile it, because nothing enforces accuracy for you.
- **Want your actual Sri Lankan tax figure to come out the other end, not just a spending summary**: this is the gap Salli is built to fill, and none of the expense trackers above attempt it.

Nothing above is mutually exclusive: plenty of people run an automated expense tracker for day-to-day spending awareness and a ledger like Salli for the tax and net-worth picture. The two solve different problems.

## What to pick

If all you want is to see where your money went, the free local apps do that well and you should use one.

If you want to know your net worth, what you'll owe the IRD, and whether a purchase is affordable against your actual goals, you need a real balance sheet and a tax engine underneath it. That is what [Salli](https://app.salli.leafmonkey.org/signup) is for: a double-entry ledger, a deterministic Sri Lankan tax engine whose [numbers never come from an AI](/blog/why-our-ai-will-never-calculate-your-tax/), and a Freedom projection that [prices purchases in months of your life](/blog/how-many-years-to-financial-independence/).

